<!--ita {"slug":"50-an-ai-use-policy-for-a-small-team","title":"An AI-use policy for a small team","category":"safety-and-judgment","excerpt":"A short sample AI-use policy for a small team, with blanks to fill in, and how to agree on it and keep it current. Not legal or HR advice.","reviewed":"2026-11-16","gated":false,"level":"intermediate","audiences":["workers-and-business-owners"],"check":["A written AI-use policy does not by itself make a tool safe; it depends on people knowing it and following it.","Data protection laws, client contracts and industry rules can set stricter limits on AI use than a team policy, and they differ by country and sector.","Work accounts for AI apps are often covered by terms the employer agreed, which can differ from personal accounts.","AI apps change their features and settings often, so a policy written for one version can go out of date."]} -->
<p>On a six-person team, one person pastes a customer's complaint into a chatbot to draft a reply. Another won't use AI at all, because nobody said it was allowed. Both are guessing.</p>
<p>A one-page policy gives them both the same answers.</p>

<h2>A sample policy to copy</h2>
<p>This sample fits a small team, like the office staff of a moving company. Copy it into a shared document and fill in each bracket. It's a starting template only, and it isn't legal or HR advice. Your local laws, contracts and industry rules may require more.</p>
[ita_prompt title="Sample AI-use policy"]
[Team name] AI-use policy
Owner: [name or role]. Last updated: [date].
Applies to: [everyone on the team, including temporary staff].
1. Apps we use: [approved apps], with [our work accounts]. Ask the owner before trying a new one.
2. Good uses: drafting, summarizing, rewording and brainstorming, plus [your team's examples].
3. Never paste: passwords and codes, ID and account numbers, [customer names and contact details] and [anything marked confidential].
4. Check before it goes out: a person reads anything sent to [customers or the public] and checks every fact, number and name.
5. Approval: no AI tool sends, pays, deletes or changes records without a person approving that action.
6. Telling people: we say when AI was used [when a customer asks, or in reports we sign], and wherever a law or contract requires it.
7. Problems: if something goes wrong, or you pasted something by mistake, tell the owner the same day. Nobody is blamed for reporting. The owner checks whether a law or contract requires telling anyone else, and by when.
8. Decisions about people: AI is not used on its own to decide about hiring, pay or discipline.
9. Review: we review this policy every [six months] and whenever our apps change.
[/ita_prompt]

<h2>How to fill in the blanks</h2>
<p>Start with what your team already does. You've probably got coworkers who use AI apps without mentioning it and others who avoid them completely. Ask both groups, and turn the real uses into line 2.</p>
<p>Keep line 3 short and concrete, so people remember it (the article "What not to paste into an AI tool" explains the categories behind it). If your work involves health, children, legal cases or people's money, your sector may have stricter rules. Ask whoever handles those rules, or a qualified advisor.</p>
<p>Line 4 needs a method people can follow on a busy day; one is in "Fact-checking an answer in five minutes". Line 5 matters as soon as an app can act by itself, for example by sending emails or updating bookings. To choose which actions need a person, see "Guardrails and human approval".</p>
<p>Name one owner, by role if people change often. That person answers questions, approves new apps and receives reports. The owner should also check whether the team uses work or personal accounts, since their terms can differ.</p>
<p>Keep the whole policy on one page, so everyone can read it in a few minutes.</p>
[ita_why title="What a written policy won't fix"]
<p>A policy doesn't make an app safe by itself. It works only if people remember it on a busy day. Line 7 says nobody is blamed for reporting, because problems you hear about can be fixed.</p>
<p>It also isn't the only rule that applies. Data protection laws, client contracts and industry rules can set stricter limits, and they differ by country and sector. AI apps change their features and settings often, too, which is why line 9 has a review date.</p>
[/ita_why]

<h2>Practice it with real situations</h2>
<p>Short examples from your own work make the rules easier to discuss in a team meeting.</p>
<p>A chatbot can draft those examples from your filled-in policy, which shouldn't contain any customer details. Put your team in the brackets of this [ita_term define="The message or instructions you type to an AI app to tell it what you want."]prompt[/ita_term]:</p>
[ita_prompt title="Situations from our policy"]
Here is our team's AI-use policy:
[paste your filled-in policy]
Write six short situations for a team of [six people in a moving company office].
In each one, someone is about to use an AI app.
After each situation, add the question: allowed, not allowed, or check with the owner first?
Put the answers, and the policy line that decides each one, at the end.
[/ita_prompt]

<p>Read the answers yourself before the meeting. Where the team can't agree which line decides a situation, rewrite that line.</p>

[ita_quiz]
[ita_q q="A coworker pasted a customer's complaint, with name and phone number, into a chatbot. What does the sample policy say to do?" answers="Tell the owner the same day; nobody is blamed for reporting|Delete the chat and say nothing, since it's gone|Ask the chatbot to forget the customer's details" correct="1" why="Line 7 asks for a same-day report, so the owner can decide what to do next."]
[ita_q q="Why name one owner in the policy?" answers="So people know who to ask about new apps and problems|So one person can check every AI answer the team writes|So there is someone to blame when something goes wrong" correct="1" why="One named person answers questions, approves new apps and receives reports."]
[ita_q q="Your team starts using an AI app that can send emails by itself. Which policy line most needs a fresh look?" answers="The list of good uses|The approval line for sending, paying or deleting|The line about telling people" correct="2" why="A tool that acts by itself needs clear approval rules for actions that are hard to undo."]
[/ita_quiz]

<h2>Try it</h2>
<ol>
<li>Copy the sample policy into a shared document.</li>
<li>Fill in the brackets you can answer alone, and put a question mark in the rest.</li>
<li>Ask two teammates what they already use AI for at work, and add those uses to line 2.</li>
<li>Run the situations request on your draft, and go through the answers together at your next team meeting.</li>
<li>Agree on a date for the first review, and write it into line 9.</li>
</ol>
